{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788172698891,"sessionID":"ses_fa89af6b3ffeNJjfALu1dal10Z","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_CpaVwq3Yr0HefJEUfb3kkGo5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n@@\n import random\n-import time\n from dataclasses import dataclass, replace\n*** Add File: /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n+# Flaky Test Root Cause\n+\n+## Behavior\n+\n+Ready tasks could appear in different orders because the scheduler shuffled the\n+ready list with the process-global random generator before sorting only by\n+priority. Retry times also varied because retry jitter came from the global\n+random generator and scheduling used wall-clock time instead of the injected\n+clock.\n+\n+## Root Cause\n+\n+`Scheduler` accepted `clock` and `random_source`, but `add`, `ready`, and\n+`schedule_retry` bypassed those dependencies. `add` made two independent\n+wall-clock calls, and `ready` introduced an unseeded shuffle. Consequently,\n+tests using fake dependencies could not control timestamps, jitter, or tie\n+ordering.\n+\n+## Fix\n+\n+The scheduler now reads the injected clock, using one timestamp for task\n+creation and the injected random source for retry jitter. Ready tasks are\n+ordered explicitly by descending priority, ascending creation time, and task\n+ID, with no random shuffle. The default behavior still uses system time and\n+the standard random module, so retry jitter remains present.\n+\n+## Verification\n+\n+- The requested `python -m pytest tests` command could not start because this\n+  environment has no `python` executable.\n+- `python3 -m pytest tests` could not start because `pytest` is not installed\n+  and `python3` has no `pip` module.\n+- `python3 -m compileall flakyqueue tests` passed.\n+- A direct Python assertion harness covering injected timestamps, stable\n+  priority/tie ordering, injected jitter, and retry readiness passed.\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\nA workspace/out/root_cause.md","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n+++ /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n@@ -1,8 +1,7 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import random\n-import time\n from dataclasses import dataclass, replace\n \n from flakyqueue.clock import SystemClock\n from flakyqueue.store import MemoryStore\n\nIndex: /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n+++ /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@\n+# Flaky Test Root Cause\n+\n+## Behavior\n+\n+Ready tasks could appear in different orders because the scheduler shuffled the\n+ready list with the process-global random generator before sorting only by\n+priority. Retry times also varied because retry jitter came from the global\n+random generator and scheduling used wall-clock time instead of the injected\n+clock.\n+\n+## Root Cause\n+\n+`Scheduler` accepted `clock` and `random_source`, but `add`, `ready`, and\n+`schedule_retry` bypassed those dependencies. `add` made two independent\n+wall-clock calls, and `ready` introduced an unseeded shuffle. Consequently,\n+tests using fake dependencies could not control timestamps, jitter, or tie\n+ordering.\n+\n+## Fix\n+\n+The scheduler now reads the injected clock, using one timestamp for task\n+creation and the injected random source for retry jitter. Ready tasks are\n+ordered explicitly by descending priority, ascending creation time, and task\n+ID, with no random shuffle. The default behavior still uses system time and\n+the standard random module, so retry jitter remains present.\n+\n+## Verification\n+\n+- The requested `python -m pytest tests` command could not start because this\n+  environment has no `python` executable.\n+- `python3 -m pytest tests` could not start because `pytest` is not installed\n+  and `python3` has no `pip` module.\n+- `python3 -m compileall flakyqueue tests` passed.\n+- A direct Python assertion harness covering injected timestamps, stable\n+  priority/tie ordering, injected jitter, and retry readiness passed.\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py","relativePath":"workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py","type":"update","patch":"Index: /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n+++ /workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\n@@ -1,8 +1,7 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import random\n-import time\n from dataclasses import dataclass, replace\n \n from flakyqueue.clock import SystemClock\n from flakyqueue.store import MemoryStore\n","additions":0,"deletions":1},{"filePath":"/workspace/out/root_cause.md","relativePath":"workspace/out/root_cause.md","type":"add","patch":"Index: /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n+++ /workspace/out/root_cause.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@\n+# Flaky Test Root Cause\n+\n+## Behavior\n+\n+Ready tasks could appear in different orders because the scheduler shuffled the\n+ready list with the process-global random generator before sorting only by\n+priority. Retry times also varied because retry jitter came from the global\n+random generator and scheduling used wall-clock time instead of the injected\n+clock.\n+\n+## Root Cause\n+\n+`Scheduler` accepted `clock` and `random_source`, but `add`, `ready`, and\n+`schedule_retry` bypassed those dependencies. `add` made two independent\n+wall-clock calls, and `ready` introduced an unseeded shuffle. Consequently,\n+tests using fake dependencies could not control timestamps, jitter, or tie\n+ordering.\n+\n+## Fix\n+\n+The scheduler now reads the injected clock, using one timestamp for task\n+creation and the injected random source for retry jitter. Ready tasks are\n+ordered explicitly by descending priority, ascending creation time, and task\n+ID, with no random shuffle. The default behavior still uses system time and\n+the standard random module, so retry jitter remains present.\n+\n+## Verification\n+\n+- The requested `python -m pytest tests` command could not start because this\n+  environment has no `python` executable.\n+- `python3 -m pytest tests` could not start because `pytest` is not installed\n+  and `python3` has no `pip` module.\n+- `python3 -m compileall flakyqueue tests` passed.\n+- A direct Python assertion harness covering injected timestamps, stable\n+  priority/tie ordering, injected jitter, and retry readiness passed.\n","additions":35,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM workspace/in/flakyqueue/flakyqueue/scheduler.py\nA workspace/out/root_cause.md","time":{"start":1788172698869,"end":1788172698881}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_099cef9f4cd57d0e016a955993688c87d2be8efb178db71fde"}},"id":"prt_05765e81c001tQUgvNK3s5QoxS","sessionID":"ses_fa89af6b3ffeNJjfALu1dal10Z","messageID":"msg_05765de27001m5OC7fWTQvD2Hb"}}
